Fetal heart rate monitoring is used in 90% of labors to assess the baby's neurological health and has been associated with a 5x increase in C-section rates. Despite decades of use, it has not decreased fetal brain injury and has been associated with an adverse trend in overall C-section rates, resulting in extremely high maternal morbidity and mortality. Electroencephalogram (EEG), measuring brain waves via electrodes, is a more accurate and earlier indicator of the baby's neurological health. However the previously proven method is invasive, requiring a scalp electrode on the baby during labor, and is not feasible at scale or in varying fetal positions. 

