Steven,"Rose, PhD, MBA","Sr. Director, Medical Affairs Strategy & Consulting (Independent Consultant -- 1099 Role)",MSLMentorLab,Founder — MSLMentorLab™ | FMOR Readiness Diagnostic + Strategy | Medical Affairs Career Clarity,"I help experienced Medical Affairs and MSL professionals reset stalled job searches by correcting role-level fit, narrative signals, and recruiter alignment — before they lose another 6–12 months pushing on the wrong doors.

Most of the people I work with are capable, credentialed, and already interviewing. What’s holding them back isn’t effort — it’s how they’re being read by the market.

I’m a retired pharma/biotech Medical Affairs leader (ARIAD, Prothena) who now supports PhDs, MDs, PharmDs, postdocs, and scientists navigating the transition into Field Medical roles.

Over 25+ years, I built and led MSL teams, hired across therapeutic areas, and worked closely with recruiters. That experience shaped FMOR — Field Medical Opportunity Readiness — a practical, recruiter-aware framework that helps scientific professionals understand:

• which Field Medical and adjacent roles they’re truly competitive for
• which bridge/on-ramp roles make sense for their background
• what signals recruiters look for
• where readiness gaps exist
• how to present their experience clearly on CVs and LinkedIn
• which warm network pathways they can leverage

How I work (FMOR — Field Medical Opportunity Readiness)

FMOR is a recruiter-aware framework I use to diagnose misalignment in role level, narrative, and expectations — particularly for experienced candidates whose searches have stalled despite interviews and strong credentials.

I use a standardized readiness assessment, a 45-minute interpretation session, and a written readout to clarify:

• realistic role fit (including bridge/on-ramp options)
• strengths and readiness signals recruiters notice
• risk areas to address
• a focused 30–60 day plan

FMOR is the framework I use to guide Search Reset engagements and other advisory work. Some people explore it independently first; others encounter it through a working session.

Start Here (FMOR Overview):
https://docs.google.com/document/d/1xqPhQPldq83CX46mAOWbYPWmmK0BCttN5bkiDG_dqj4/preview

