Jeffery,Mays,Managing Partner,Nissan of Stockton,Owner-Multiple Automotive Dealerships/Dealer-Adjacent Businesses,"My Beginnings - It would have been really cool if I had the advantages of the internet, where you can share your message with large audiences; but that wasn’t the case back when I started working right out of school back in 1983. I received what you’d call a ""real-life education."" Selling radio advertising, I was given a product packet, and then sent out to hit the streets. The packet contained: a list of clients, available packages to sell, and the suggested prices for which to sell them. At this point, I hadn’t been taught any formal sales techniques or received any training. I was told “good luck” and sent on my way. 

I was forced to learn on the go, and honestly, it was a blessing in disguise. I learned quickly how to interact with people, get comfortable hearing rejections, how to manage my time, how to prospect and give quality presentations. All these experiences laid the foundation for my future success in the automotive industry. When I first started out, and on the road for so long, even though it came with many rejections, even the small wins felt like huge accomplishments. This trial by fire, real-life education prepares you to be ready for anything.  

The Outside-In Perspective - The Outside In Perspective is a method that I’ve learned during my time working in the automotive industry. I am constantly thinking about: How do our facility surroundings and energy/vibe look to customers as a first impression vs. what our staff is used to looking at every day, or from the ""Inside-out."" What resonates with my potential clients? What type of services do they need? What level of service are they expecting? Basically, I think about everything from the customer's perspective, and then give them the experience I think they expect. Prior to the internet, a customer’s experience would start when they entered the dealership. Now it starts well before that on the many virtual platforms that customers search.

The Outside-In perspective allows dealerships to focus on what we know really matters…the customer and the teammates. In the current car business climate, customers are interacting with several dealerships via the internet or the phone before even making their way into a dealership; therefore, we don’t try to work the sales process to benefit us “inside” the dealership. We remain cognizant of the fact that we need to look “outside” from the customer's point of view. 

Always aware-Always act
