Dr. Birklein has received training at a variety of institutions around the globe, including the Institute for Critical Psychology at the Freie University in Berlin (BA), the Fritz Perls Institute in Germany, the Alice Solomon University in Berlin (Social Work/ Pedagogics), Antioch Graduate School, (MA), The New School for Social Research (MA, PhD), and postgraduate training at the William Alanson White Institute and the NYU Postdoctoral Program for Psychotherapy and Psychoanalysis. She also holds a degree in Dance, Performance Arts, and Dance Education from Berlin, a certificate in Laban Movement Analysis (LMA) and the Kestenberg Movement Profile (KMP), and trained in many somatic practices and approaches.‍

Her research focus is the inter-generational transmission of affect, the body, and movement in psychotherapy and psychoanalysis, particularly on trauma, PTSD, and the nonverbal/non–semantic aspects in the clinical setting. ‍Dr. Birklein specializes in Relational Psychoanalysis and Body and Movement/Somatic practices in psychoanalytic contexts and offers psychodynamic, relational psychotherapy, psychoanalysis, and body/movement–informed psychotherapy. ‍

