"Dr. Leonard E,","Weisman, M.D.","Founder, and Chief Technology Officer",LabReady,Founder at LabReady | US Army Medical Colonel Veteran | Physician and Neonatologist  40+ Years | Inventor of First Anti-Virals for RSV |  260 Publications | West Point Graduate,"Dr. Lenny Weisman is a distinguished physician with 45 years of experience, a retired US Army Colonel with 24 years of service, a second-generation Holocaust survivor, and one of the world's leading neonatologists.

As a neonatologist for 40 years, Dr. Weisman witnessed the tragic loss of babies due to misdiagnosis. Realizing the root cause was in how samples were transported, he ""retired"" from clinical practice to develop LabReady® Automated Sample Prep—the first regulatory-approved specimen transport system featuring specimen multiplier technology. This innovation eliminates the need for traditional transport, significantly reducing misdiagnosis. Currently, Dr. Weisman is pioneering qPCR-AI—a groundbreaking rapid qPCR multiplex diagnostic platform that combines PCR and edge-AI to enable automatic diagnosis of infections in physicians' offices, ERs, hospitals, and pharmacies.
 
Born in a refugee camp in Germany at the end of World War II, Dr. Weisman is the son of Holocaust survivors of multiple camps including Auschwitz. He was brought to the United States through a foundation and later recruited to West Point, where he graduated with honors. After completing Airborne, Ranger, and Air Defense Artillery schools, he served as an academic neonatologist in the Army, working with Sioux Oglala and MHA Nation patients. During this time, he discovered and developed the first two antiviral drugs for Respiratory Syncytial Virus, which became the breakthrough drugs for MedImmune, eventually sold to AstraZeneca for $15.6 billion.

After retiring from the Army, Dr. Weisman joined Baylor College of Medicine, where he served as Chief of the Newborn Service at Texas Children’s Hospital. He built the largest neonatal unit in the United States and envisioned the creation of a women’s hospital, now the Women’s Pavilion. While at TCH, he spearheaded the birth and care of the world’s first surviving set of octuplets, gaining recognition from The New York Times, CNN, and other international media outlets. Dr. Weisman also pioneered numerous globally adopted protocols, including the Texas Children's Kangaroo Crew®, which has been transporting critically ill babies and children for over 20 years and performs more than 1,500 critical transports annually.

Today, Dr. Weisman is commercializing the breakthrough MyLabReady qPCR-AI platform. The first pocket-sized, onboard-AI, and true rapid multiplex qPCR diagnostic platform, designed to automate human sample testing of symptomatic, at-risk patients in any healthcare setting, starting with infections.",Yes,,,,,,United States,Texas,Houston,founder,"master_information_technology, c_suite, medical_health",https://www.linkedin.com/in/leonard-weisman-m-d-b6793094,LabReady,2-10,6,medical equipment manufacturing,,"LabReady is freeing multiplex qPCR molecular diagnostics from traditional labs and into the hands of farmers, factory owners and healthcare providers. Introducing MyLabReady, the groundbreaking qPCR + AI diagnostic technology revolutionizing infection testing. Designed for simplicity, MyLabReady brings AI-enabled and lab-grade multi-pathogen qPCR testing directly to your practice, emergency room, urgent care, or pharmacy delivering results in minutes with unparalleled accuracy.",http://www.labready.com,https://www.linkedin.com/company/labreadydx,PRIVATELY_HELD,0,100000-500000,,,labready.com,,,Healthcare founders/owners netnew
